## Choices

The practical result is one command, `view --input ABS [--json]`. It reads one strict `desk-state/1` record and emits a `desk-view/1` view. The view shows the desk goal, every declared lane, returns that need review, unresolved blockers, and the recorded Prime handoff. `elected` is always null. A worker label is a label. A null worker stays unrecorded. The module does not infer that an absent worker is available, and it does not pick a winner by page number.

Commit observations and live inspections stay separate. The module shells out to the pinned reader, inspector, and brief after the pin check. It does not import them and does not edit them. The brief is asked to inspect only when the record says `inspect-named-members`. Coordination is supplied and source-bound. A different source hash stays unprojected. Judgments are not rebound to new bytes.

The `05.12` coordination is the accepted 05.14 record reused because the blob hash still matches. The `05.18` and `05.20` coordination records are supplied for this rehearsal from those pages' pointer text and the accepted review pages. Reader ambiguities, including `duplicate-release` on `05.18`, stay visible. Terminal completion is not inferred from an accepted review. The recorded next step is the Prime sentence in `RECONCILIATION.md`: one final prepared test bundle and a Director-led trial. D1 stays unresolved. The packet registry cited there is a Prime proposal, not a Director lane.

The example `desk.json` is a proposed working configuration. It is not a second assignment. `render_live.py` does not read this view. No migration runs.

Exit 0 means the view has no attention item and no unknown. Exit 1 means attention or an unknown is visible. Exit 2 means the record, the pin, the path, or the output is refused. Reader legacy codes are shown and do not by themselves change the exit. This note does not contain a receipt.
